  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\n...FLOOD WATCH IN EFFECT FROM MONDAY AFTERNOON THROUGH WEDNESDAY \nMORNING...\n\nTwo weather features to focus on in the short term are the upper \nlow currently centered over northern California and Hurricane Polo\nspinning off the Baja California coast. Tropical steering pattern\nis favorable for Polo to drift north on Monday, making likely \nlandfall over Baja California Sur during the evening hours. At the\nsame time, the mid-latitude Pacific low aloft will make a \nsoutheast dive down to the Colorado River Valley, forced by a \nstrengthening 75-knot jet streak on its backside. A weakening Polo\nwill latch onto this stronger mid-latitude flow, shifting it \nnortheast into Sonora/Chihuahua and straight toward El Paso on\nTuesday.\n\nMonday will feature a rapid moisture increase locally, thanks to \nthe approaching tropical remnants. Veering of mid-level winds to \nthe south ahead of the Pacific low will pull in deep, tropically-\nsourced moisture into southern New Mexico and far west Texas. \nPrecipitable water values, measured at just 1.1\" this evening \n(00Z KEPZ RAOB), will spike to 1.5-1.6\" Monday evening. This may \nbreak seasonal moisture records for El Paso, and is easily 3 to 4 \nstandard deviations higher than usual for the end of the monsoon \nseason. This moisture-rich atmosphere, combined with PVA and \nincreasing diffluent flow aloft, will lead to widespread showers \nand thunderstorms area wide starting as early as Monday afternoon.\n\nGood confidence in rain for all local zones Monday night through \nWednesday morning as the upper low pulls up tropical moisture like\na fire hose into New Mexico and West Texas. 48-hour rainfall \nforecasts suggest widespread totals ranging from 1.00-2.00\". NBM \nguidance backs this up. Stronger storms and training (or multiple \nrounds) of rain showers over the same areas will lead to localized\n3-4\". Soil saturation is very high right now, so rain will lead \nto rapid runoff and flash flooding quickly. A Moderate (Level 3 of\n4) Risk of flash flooding is in place almost area-wide for \nTuesday. Of particular concern for me will be the Sacramento \nMountains, which have already had an above-normal precipitation \nseason and significant flooding over the past two weeks. A Flood\nWatch is in effect area-wide from Monday afternoon through\nWednesday morning. WFO El Paso will be proactive with flood \nwarnings in areas with recent heavy rains if needed Tuesday- \nWednesday.\n\nOn top of the heavy rain risk, the passing tropical depression \nmay still have some stronger winds associated with it. 00Z HRRR \nrun tonight was of particular concern, and recent NAM runs have \nalso picked up on the potential for gusty conditions Tuesday \nevening as Polo moves through. Increased forecasted winds for \nTuesday afternoon/evening, especially for El Paso, Hudspeth, Otero\ncounties where strong winds out of the south just ahead of the \nanticipated storm track could lead to gusts in the 30-40 mph \nrange.\n\nBoth GFS and ECMWF global models show a track of the upper low \nprogressing across central NM on Wednesday. Rain showers will \nslowly decrease in coverage Wednesday morning, with the remnants \nof Polo quickly dissipating. Subsident \"dry-slotting\" at the base \nof the low will lead to drier weather for our area by Wednesday \nafternoon.\n\nOnce all this action passes, it looks like a fairly \"boring\" \nweather pattern for the start of October. Strengthening upper high\nover the U.S. Southwest will usher in a much-needed break from \nrainfall. Continued east surface flow could keep temperatures \nbelow normal through the rest of the week. Lowland highs lingering\nin the upper 70s into next weekend seems like the best bet right \nnow.",
